<edge source="bff67a86-bdb2-4ccc-9c13-8e25d42f6ad3" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">When a misstatement of sex results in higher-than-necessary premiums, the insurer refunds the surplus directly tied to the discrepancy in the calculated death benefit, ensuring fairness relative to the actual risk profile.</data>
  <data key="d4">affected by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bff67a86-bdb2-4ccc-9c13-8e25d42f6ad3" target="b6c70d4a-bb66-4ce6-8a7e-fcce14499c75">
  <data key="d3">Overpayment of premiums may occur if the insured's sex was incorrectly stated; since premiums are tied to risk assessment, corrections lead to adjustments, including refunds based on prior payments required by the policyholder.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bff67a86-bdb2-4ccc-9c13-8e25d42f6ad3" target="474ef3ff-fa30-4661-8d2d-93b34f04b312">
  <data key="d3">Because the policy’s full scope includes all official documents, any adjustment arising from a misstatement of sex falls under the company’s obligation to correct payments per the agreed-upon contract terms, making the procedural outcome contingent on the initial definition of the contract.</data>
  <data key="d4">enforces</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bff67a86-bdb2-4ccc-9c13-8e25d42f6ad3" target="2ff2e286-b2f4-46d0-a585-be514606308f">
  <data key="d3">When a misstatement regarding sex results in higher-than-appropriate premiums being paid, the policy allows for a refund of the surplus amount to the policyholder. This reflects the principle established earlier—that such claims are treated as representational errors, not breaches requiring penalties.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bff67a86-bdb2-4ccc-9c13-8e25d42f6ad3" target="a0e459f4-ff70-494b-bb20-e24bfe587be4">
  <data key="d3">When a misstatement of sex results in overpaid premiums, the insurer must issue a refund, reinforcing the need for accuracy in applications. This obligation depends on the validity of information originally submitted, aligning with the rule that only application details can form legal grounds for disputes.</data>
  <data key="d4">enforces limitation</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bff67a86-bdb2-4ccc-9c13-8e25d42f6ad3" target="17a08a71-b8b3-4e8a-85f9-e97555d901c8">
  <data key="d3">When a misstatement of sex causes overpayment of premiums, the insurer must refund the surplus. To validate such a determination, the original application—which must be attached per fact_sentence_1—serves as foundational evidence.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bff67a86-bdb2-4ccc-9c13-8e25d42f6ad3" target="9d18139a-0c83-4f90-b974-a4a7e8663000">
  <data key="d3">When a misstatement of sex causes overpaid premiums, the company refunds the difference—a process enabled by the policy’s formal framework, not individual agents’ discretion, as emphasized in the first statement.</data>
  <data key="d4">follows from rule</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bff67a86-bdb2-4ccc-9c13-8e25d42f6ad3" target="c7c925cb-8c15-4c3e-98c7-37dd24303a91">
  <data key="d3">A refund of overpaid premiums occurs only if correcting a misstatement of sex causes the actual premiums paid to exceed those required at the corrected rate. This situation arises directly from adjustments made to the death benefit based on accurate demographic information.</data>
  <data key="d4">result of</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bff67a86-bdb2-4ccc-9c13-8e25d42f6ad3" target="00981cfe-a248-4489-99ff-6dff511e9bd7">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 2 covers scenarios involving misstatements of sex causing premium overpayment, mandating a refund without interest. This mirrors fact sentence 1’s handling of age misstatements, ensuring equitable treatment regardless of whether the error pertains to age or sex.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bff67a86-bdb2-4ccc-9c13-8e25d42f6ad3" target="6a1e6299-7170-452e-85d0-d0ae6f09cb82">
  <data key="d3">When a misstatement of sex causes an overpayment of premiums, the insurer refunds the excess amount. This outcome implies that any shortfall in premium payments would trigger a different response—namely, a reduction in the death benefit—to maintain actuarial balance.</data>
  <data key="d4">complementary outcome</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
