<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="6c933248-145a-4629-9244-b471149ae619">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums being deducted from the death benefit implies that such defaults have occurred earlier, likely resulting in policy lapse if not resolved during the grace period. This outcome depends on timely premium payments, which prevent termination.</data>
  <data key="d4">result of</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="dc295506-9994-4bc6-8410-24347075b1d6">
  <data key="d3">The deduction of unpaid premiums from the death benefit only applies after the policy has lapsed due to nonpayment, meaning the loss of coverage precedes this financial adjustment.</data>
  <data key="d4">result of</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="38763c7f-abef-4ce9-83af-a9a6ee8c0390">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums reduce the death benefit when the insured passes away while the policy is active. This implies the policy had not lapsed, thus excluding scenarios covered by the "no benefits" rule stated in the first fact.</data>
  <data key="d4">prevents lapse implication</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="f132426a-4a89-442f-804e-9685b2a8370a">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums are subtracted from the death benefit when the insured passes away, reflecting an alternative consequence compared to paying them with interest during reinstatement after lapse.</data>
  <data key="d4">alternative consequence</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="3c433250-298a-4a66-9011-6ba31393c55a">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums are settled by deducting them from the death benefit, meaning reinstatement cannot fully erase past financial liabilities affecting payouts.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="2a08c0b6-2e7e-4895-939b-cc9797b12f18">
  <data key="d3">The deduction of unpaid premiums from the death benefit depends on the policy being active at the time of death, meaning the death benefit is only applicable if the policy was still in force.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="8453f6b0-fb6e-4aa6-ab20-20d1e0198fd4">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums are deducted from the death benefit because this deduction is mandated by the policy’s governing terms and conditions.</data>
  <data key="d4">implied by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="2474d284-5bd2-401c-91b1-36a9a5e512cb">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums are subtracted from the death benefit, which is initially set equal to the face amount stated in the policy specifications.</data>
  <data key="d4">deducted from</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="1ad14f44-4ab9-4865-bd44-65dbcba3e242">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums reduce the death benefit only after the insurer confirms the insured's death through official documentation.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="b8c541a1-08d6-4493-9d12-7e4b17736e50">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums are subtracted from the death benefit before it's distributed. This deduction occurs alongside other financial obligations like indebtedness that also reduce the total payout.</data>
  <data key="d4">combines with</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="0eab6e49-c223-4495-87bb-58f1202750b4">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums reduce the death benefit when a claim is processed, ensuring accurate disbursement based on policy terms. This deduction occurs after verification of death through official documents like a certified death certificate.</data>
  <data key="d4">compliance with</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="79ffd32a-ede6-403a-ac15-f636b8aa494f">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums reduce the death benefit before distribution, affecting the total amount available for the beneficiary. This reduction may influence whether the beneficiary chooses an alternative payment option based on the remaining payout size.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="a807a94a-e844-41c7-ba21-2154a3872ba5">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums are subtracted from the death benefit prior to any interest being applied, reducing the base amount upon which interest accrues.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="81fd1296-c3bf-4581-b20f-126e5d595425">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums reduce the death benefit available for payout, which affects the final amount distributed once the insurer approves the claim documentation.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="a49cafd1-8d48-414f-8488-d5ff6730e3d9">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums affecting the death benefit depend on whether the policy remained active through the grace period, making the grace period condition crucial to determining liability.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="9aff7508-84dd-468d-83c6-658c086cf16c">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums that lead to a reduction in the death benefit imply that failure to pay during the grace period results in both lapse and diminished payouts, reinforcing the importance of timely premium payments.</data>
  <data key="d4">result of</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="0dd1735c-ba92-4588-9e4a-6408b636a844">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums, which affect the death benefit, must be settled during reinstatement, making the reinstatement process dependent on resolving those arrears.</data>
  <data key="d4">required for</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums directly affect the final death benefit, so the amount originally specified in the policy must account for potential reductions due to these liabilities.</data>
  <data key="d4">affects calculation</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="ae130c33-876d-4d39-9ee6-6b6250047099">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums reduce the death benefit directly (fact_sentence_2), and when combined with benefits recalculated due to age misstatement (fact_sentence_1), result in further reductions beyond standard deductions, highlighting cumulative impacts on claim payouts.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="c7c925cb-8c15-4c3e-98c7-37dd24303a91">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums reduce the death benefit, which may already have been recalculated lower due to a misstated sex classification.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="9a6577b2-6763-4f14-9b38-f89dde765452">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums diminish the death benefit, making the 31-day grace period critical—its expiration may trigger loss of coverage unless timely action is taken to avoid such deductions.</data>
  <data key="d4">result of</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="d822587b-a5be-4232-b595-d6f428b09987">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums—such as those not covered by the $420 annual rate—are subtracted from the death benefit, meaning the stated premium amount directly affects the net payout under the policy’s terms.</data>
  <data key="d4">impacted by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="3af0e3a5-6ae4-44d2-9986-8f2a02aac638">
  <data key="d3">The presence of Taylor Ellis as beneficiary means she is entitled to receive the adjusted death benefit after deductions for unpaid premiums.</data>
  <data key="d4">affects beneficiary payout</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="e95a83d3-c700-45d5-abe2-2438276d3c5e">
  <data key="d3">Unpaid premiums reduce the death benefit available to Taylor Ellis, who is the designated beneficiary, thereby diminishing the financial outcome she would receive upon the insured's passing.</data>
  <data key="d4">affects beneficiary</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="45eb8281-3543-4e01-bd76-8cddd037b578" target="6c0901ed-f52a-4ce9-9d52-9547016ca265">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 explains how unpaid premiums affect payouts, making it relevant to the longevity and integrity of the coverage window described in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
