  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">payment</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="8af1e7aa-a2ee-45ee-874e-fc91ce2a0490">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">rate</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="2a08c0b6-2e7e-4895-939b-cc9797b12f18">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a contingent beneficiary depends on the absence of a surviving designated beneficiary after the insured’s death. This secondary arrangement only applies if the primary beneficiary cannot receive the benefits due to prior death.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="8453f6b0-fb6e-4aa6-ab20-20d1e0198fd4">
  <data key="d3">When no designated beneficiary survives the insured, the policy’s terms dictate that proceeds go to the contingent beneficiary, reflecting a key provision governing death benefit disbursement.</data>
  <data key="d4">implied by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="1ad14f44-4ab9-4865-bd44-65dbcba3e242">
  <data key="d3">Beneficiary designation affects how proceeds are distributed once the insurer confirms death. This step occurs only after proof of death has been accepted.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="0eab6e49-c223-4495-87bb-58f1202750b4">
  <data key="d3">The absence of surviving primary beneficiaries triggers payout to contingent beneficiaries, necessitating verified proof such as a certified death certificate to initiate the process.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="81fd1296-c3bf-4581-b20f-126e5d595425">
  <data key="d3">When no primary beneficiary survives the insured, the proceeds are directed to the contingent beneficiary, triggering the obligation for prompt payment under the claims procedure outlined in the prior statement.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="9f7028fc-d597-413b-ae31-675f57f9e024">
  <data key="d3">When the primary beneficiary does not survive the insured, the policy directs payments to the contingent beneficiary. If that individual also fails to survive, the proceeds are distributed to an unnamed recipient.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="a49cafd1-8d48-414f-8488-d5ff6730e3d9">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a contingency plan for beneficiary payouts depends on the policy remaining in force; thus, maintaining coverage through the grace period enables the execution of the procedure described in fact_sentence_2.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="9aff7508-84dd-468d-83c6-658c086cf16c">
  <data key="d3">While fact_sentence_2 describes what happens to proceeds if no primary beneficiary survives, it assumes the policy was active at time of death—meaning it did not lapse due to nonpayment during the grace period.</data>
  <data key="d4">precondition</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="0dd1735c-ba92-4588-9e4a-6408b636a844">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a validly reinstated policy allows for proper disbursement of proceeds to beneficiaries as specified, making reinstatement necessary for the effective implementation of the beneficiary clause detailed in fact_sentence_2.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">The rule governing payment to contingent beneficiaries assumes a predefined death benefit amount established by the policy specifications, making the outcome dependent on the initially set payout figure.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="9a6577b2-6763-4f14-9b38-f89dde765452">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a contingent beneficiary provision depends on the policy remaining active at time of death; the 31-day grace period helps preserve that activity, making it relevant to whether the contingent beneficiary might ultimately receive funds.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="d822587b-a5be-4232-b595-d6f428b09987">
  <data key="d3">A surviving contingent beneficiary only receives proceeds if the policy remains active through timely premium payments, such as the $420 annual premium specified.</data>
  <data key="d4">required for</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="3af0e3a5-6ae4-44d2-9986-8f2a02aac638">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a contingent beneficiary clause supports the validity of naming Taylor Ellis as the primary beneficiary, ensuring continuity of payout even if the primary recipient predeceases the insured.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="e95a83d3-c700-45d5-abe2-2438276d3c5e">
  <data key="d3">The provision that proceeds go to a contingent beneficiary if no primary one survives depends on having a clearly designated primary beneficiary, such as Taylor Ellis mentioned in fact_sentence_1, making the two statements logically interdependent.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="6c0901ed-f52a-4ce9-9d52-9547016ca265">
  <data key="d3">The rule that proceeds go to a contingent beneficiary if the primary one doesn’t survive relies on the existence of an active policy, such as the one covering the period from August 1, 2025, to July 31, 2045.</data>
  <data key="d4">contextual support</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 establishes the default path for benefit disbursement when the primary beneficiary doesn’t survive, while fact_sentence_2 provides a rule for resolving ambiguity in death timing—presuming the beneficiary died first—which directly supports the application of the contingent beneficiary clause.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">When the primary beneficiary is presumed to have predeceased the insured due to lack of clear survivorship evidence, the contingency clause in fact_sentence_1 activates, allowing proceeds to flow to the contingent beneficiary.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d3">The rule about paying proceeds to a contingent beneficiary depends on the ability to designate such a person, which can be restricted if an irrevocable beneficiary exists and cannot be removed without consent.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d3">The rule about contingent beneficiaries applies only after primary beneficiaries fail to survive, whereas the irrevocable beneficiary clause limits ownership control regardless of survival status. Together, they shape how beneficiary designations influence claim disbursement under various conditions.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 outlines the default payout path when primary beneficiaries do not survive, which depends on whether a contingent beneficiary was named—a detail influenced by the ability to designate such parties discussed in fact_sentence_2.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">The rule about contingent beneficiaries depends on timely updates to beneficiary designations, meaning changes must occur before death to ensure correct payout routing.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 1 states that if no designated beneficiary survives the insured, proceeds go to the contingent beneficiary if one exists. This is directly mirrored in fact sentence 2, which repeats the exact same condition and outcome, showing full alignment.</data>
  <data key="d4">identical to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">1.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">When no primary beneficiary survives the insured, proceeds go to the contingent beneficiary per fact_sentence_1; if that fails due to unlocatable beneficiaries, fact_sentence_2 enables transfer to the estate after 90 days.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">The rule about paying proceeds to a contingent beneficiary assumes that beneficiary designations remain valid; however, fact_sentence_2 clarifies that new beneficiary assignments can override prior ones even after death, potentially altering who qualifies as the "contingent" recipient under fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 outlines a clear hierarchy of beneficiary designation, prioritizing contingent beneficiaries. Fact_sentence_2 broadens this by allowing the insurer to distribute funds to individuals considered equitably entitled when no valid beneficiary remains, serving as a legal safety net.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="92a0c3fd-b6dc-4a3b-852d-2dde74d5850f">
  <data key="d3">The beneficiary distribution rule relies on the policy’s overall legal robustness, ensured by the severability provision stated in fact_sentence_2.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="14d9f380-89b3-451f-94aa-1ef90e6286fa">
  <data key="d3">The rule governing beneficiary payouts depends on the policy being recognized as the definitive agreement, ensuring such clauses are legally enforceable and cannot be altered by external factors.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="00afa11e-1a32-400d-a440-38b9111578d0">
  <data key="d3">The beneficiary succession rule depends on the policy’s overall legal validity; if the entire policy were voided due to invalidity, the contingent beneficiary clause would become irrelevant. However, the severability clause protects such provisions by preserving them even if unrelated parts fail.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="66d34f7f-f03b-44b9-ae46-16416415bc2a">
  <data key="d3">The rule requiring written waivers ensures that beneficiary designations cannot be altered informally, preserving the intended distribution path outlined in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b" target="a2f514f9-483a-46e4-bd60-a528a1b42c8b">
  <data key="d3">The rule about contingent beneficiaries depends on the integrity of policy terms, including the requirement for written approval to waive provisions like designation clauses. Without such formalities, beneficiary assignments might be challenged.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="29379de1-6cf5-4371-beb9-b35bbfdc0b6b" target="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">governed by</data>
</edge>
