<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="f5e8830b-8202-441f-8eea-c6abab133fdb">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="9ab0ecce-83bb-49b7-a2b4-83f601a713b6">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="55ecb2bc-3eb1-4c24-8467-aac52d259acc">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="459d00a2-50cb-4a03-9d5b-cd2f0406b162">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">date</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">A beneficiary is defined as the individual(s) chosen by the policyholder to receive the death benefit. This designation must be documented in writing and officially filed with the insurance company to be effective.</data>
  <data key="d4">requires documentation</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d3">When multiple beneficiaries are named without specified shares, the principle established in the definition of a beneficiary—that they are entitled to the death benefit—is applied through equal division.</data>
  <data key="d4">exemplifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">Knowing who the beneficiary is enables the policyholder to make informed decisions about modifying the designation. This establishes the basis for the flexibility in updating beneficiaries under most circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">Knowing who the beneficiary is enables clarity around changing them. A change only takes legal effect once formally documented through a signed request.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">The definition of a beneficiary establishes the foundation for understanding how designations can be altered. Even after the insured’s death, such changes remain enforceable under company procedures.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d3">The definition of a beneficiary establishes the foundation for understanding why certain restrictions apply—such as requiring consent for changes if the beneficiary is irrevocable—to protect the intended recipient from unilateral alteration.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">The primary beneficiary, defined in sentence one, receives the death benefit only if they outlive the insured; otherwise, the proceeds pass to the contingent beneficiary as stated in sentence two.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d3">The definition of a beneficiary sets the foundation for understanding why certain restrictions apply; specifically, once a beneficiary is named as irrevocable, changes require their consent due to legal protections granted to them.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">The designation of a beneficiary establishes the initial recipient of life insurance proceeds; if that individual does not survive the insured, the funds proceed to the next tier in the distribution chain—the estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">Given that the beneficiary is defined as the recipient of the death benefit, if both the insured and beneficiary die at the same time, legal rules apply to determine precedence—specifically, the beneficiary is presumed to have died first unless overridden by state law.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">The definition of a beneficiary sets up the context for applying the legal presumption regarding death order. Without understanding who the beneficiary is, the rule about presuming their prior death cannot be meaningfully applied.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5">
  <data key="d3">The definition of a beneficiary sets up the context for determining inheritance rights; if the order of death is unclear, the law presumes the beneficiary predeceased the insured, affecting distribution eligibility.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">If the designated beneficiary does not survive the insured, the death benefit cannot be paid to them, leading to the default distribution to the insured’s estate as outlined in the policy terms.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary survives the insured, the insurer has authority to determine who should receive the death benefit based on equity, extending beyond formal designations.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional outcome</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary is designated under the policy, they may choose to disclaim the death benefit, triggering a legal assumption that they predeceased the insured. This ensures proper allocation of funds according to secondary beneficiaries or the estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">disclaims → predecedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">If the named beneficiary cannot be identified or contacted within 90 days after the insured’s death, the insurer defaults to paying the death benefit to the estate, based on the initial designation established in the first sentence.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">If the named beneficiary cannot receive the death benefit due to lack of eligibility or simultaneous death, the insurer fulfills its obligation by paying the estate or an equitable claimant, thus closing its responsibility under the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4">discharges</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f">
  <data key="d3">The definition of a beneficiary establishes the primary recipients of death benefits, setting the stage for exceptions like those described in fact 2, where non-beneficiaries may claim based on equitable grounds.</data>
  <data key="d4">extends</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">Knowing the beneficiary ensures proper distribution of the pre-defined death benefit amount outlined in the policy specifications, completing the payout framework.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="3af0e3a5-6ae4-44d2-9986-8f2a02aac638">
  <data key="d3">The concept of a beneficiary receiving the death benefit is exemplified by Taylor Ellis being listed as such in the policy described in chunk summary 1.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="df71832b-c637-4428-9661-6f164f3b5b9f">
  <data key="d3">The concept of a beneficiary outlined in fact_sentence_2 is exemplified by Taylor Ellis, whose designation as the beneficiary in fact_sentence_1 illustrates its practical application in real-life policy structures.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="26fb45c6-1b02-446d-aa67-f95ab9f3bac0">
  <data key="d3">Knowing that the beneficiary is Taylor Ellis allows interpretation of the governing law clause, as NY State statutes determine whether such designations override estate claims or affect distribution priority.</data>
  <data key="d4">contextualizes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474" target="49ede6cd-0ba5-4ff3-af79-5ed128947216">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 establishes the universal rule behind the specific case presented in fact_sentence_1, clarifying that beneficiaries are chosen by the policy owner—here, Jordan Ellis—as part of standard policy design.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="367b9688-6444-41cd-a240-4d42ee0dac03" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">receives death benefit from</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f5e8830b-8202-441f-8eea-c6abab133fdb" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">is received by</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="7c7e3ee0-11e6-4379-bfd1-0881db4e66f9">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="12ce42cb-56bf-4ccd-95fa-ae96aa5a8328">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="cf6a6067-3d30-4b1d-be62-2b4dab4a27a0">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="bc85f8c6-779e-4720-9915-91753376943a">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="38bc3aaa-4db1-4f75-9fb2-ae1269410565">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">organization</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="597ba74c-4eca-4154-9b48-a9ea7b75dd35">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="8f229b10-709d-40ed-9fe9-0deeda84cb2a">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="ff657ef7-efcb-479e-975e-8e3d48379fbd">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="9faa3342-14b6-453f-a799-2f553870dc86">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">date</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">To be recognized as a beneficiary, an individual must be formally named through a written document submitted to the insurance company; without such documentation, the designation lacks legal standing.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables validity</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d3">Without a written and filed beneficiary designation, the process outlined in fact_sentence_2—equal sharing among unnamed beneficiaries—cannot apply, as there would be no formal list of recipients.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">A written designation of a beneficiary is required before changes can occur. This prerequisite allows the policyholder to modify the beneficiary while the insured is alive, unless the beneficiary is irrevocable.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">The need for a written designation ensures that any subsequent change is formally recorded. A change becomes effective only once the written request is signed, confirming compliance with the initial documentation rule.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">A written and filed beneficiary designation ensures that the distribution path is clear; without such a designation, the insurer defaults to paying proceeds to the contingent beneficiary if one exists.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">The requirement to file beneficiary designations in writing ensures that any subsequent changes—even those recorded after the insured’s death—are legally recognized and enforceable, maintaining administrative validity regardless of timing relative to death.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d3">The requirement that a beneficiary be designated in writing enables the legal recognition of irrevocable beneficiaries, whose status necessitates explicit consent before any changes can occur.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">A written and filed beneficiary designation ensures that the intended recipient receives proceeds; without one, or if the primary and contingent beneficiaries predecease the insured, the policy proceeds pass to the estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d3">The requirement to designate a beneficiary in writing sets the foundation for establishing legal rights, including those tied to irrevocability. This formality enables the enforceability of conditions like needing consent from an irrevocable beneficiary before assigning the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">A written beneficiary designation ensures the company has clear documentation to invoke its default assumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured if death timing is ambiguous.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">A written beneficiary designation is required to establish eligibility; without one, the legal presumption regarding the order of death becomes applicable under intestacy rules.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">A written beneficiary designation prevents automatic payout to the estate by ensuring clear intent. Without it, the company has no legal basis to assign proceeds elsewhere, leading to payment to the estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">prevents default</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5">
  <data key="d3">A written beneficiary designation ensures that the legal presumption about death order only applies if a valid beneficiary existed at the time of death. Without proper documentation, the presumption cannot be invoked.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary is properly designated in writing with the company, they retain the right to accept or disclaim the proceeds. Disclaiming the proceeds triggers a legal presumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f">
  <data key="d3">If no valid beneficiary is designated through proper documentation, alternative equitable claims may arise, including individuals who covered end-of-life or funeral costs.</data>
  <data key="d4">fallback to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">Without a validly designated beneficiary confirmed via written filing, the life insurance company assumes authority over benefit distribution, enabling it to identify individuals equitably entitled to receive proceeds under absence-of-beneficiary conditions.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary is not formally designated in writing with the insurer, or cannot be located within 90 days post-death, the policy proceeds default to the estate as an alternate recipient.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary is not properly designated in writing and filed with the company, the insurer may pay proceeds to the estate or an equitable claimant, thereby discharging its obligation under the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">A properly designated beneficiary ensures that the explicitly stated death benefit from the policy specifications is paid out accordingly, making the existence of both a confirmed benefit amount and a valid beneficiary essential for claim fulfillment.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="7c7e3ee0-11e6-4379-bfd1-0881db4e66f9" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">designates</data>
</edge>
<edge source="12ce42cb-56bf-4ccd-95fa-ae96aa5a8328" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">is designated</data>
</edge>
<edge source="cf6a6067-3d30-4b1d-be62-2b4dab4a27a0" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">is designated as secondary</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc85f8c6-779e-4720-9915-91753376943a" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">authorizes or initiates designation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="38bc3aaa-4db1-4f75-9fb2-ae1269410565" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">receives and files written designation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="597ba74c-4eca-4154-9b48-a9ea7b75dd35" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">is paid to</data>
</edge>
<edge source="8f229b10-709d-40ed-9fe9-0deeda84cb2a" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">scenario requiring prior written beneficiary designation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ff657ef7-efcb-479e-975e-8e3d48379fbd" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">requires written designation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9faa3342-14b6-453f-a799-2f553870dc86" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">timeframe for filing written designation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="f09d63bc-646e-4cf6-9045-a84c456eacf6">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="1e3d86f2-cdad-4c30-a947-fc027412cc48">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="ae106efa-30b6-4f6f-8c81-73f43667c639">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="7dd5fccc-3591-4e46-a046-385520d271de">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="dbe7d497-d868-4da1-95d0-620f81a79fec">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">organization</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="9cb4ce7f-9577-4146-abdc-61f07ee5f5f7">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="6531c597-7775-413a-93a5-b6179104a6a8">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="60dc4a66-920a-4d9b-a26f-61f0893e156d">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="7fe27cca-101b-45d0-bb5a-ac0bcb0365c2">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">date</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">The rule requiring equal distribution among unnamed co-beneficiaries stems directly from the foundational concept that each beneficiary has an entitlement to the death benefit as defined by the policyholder.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">The rule about equal shares applies only after beneficiaries are formally designated; thus, the need for written filing precedes any consideration of distribution proportions.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">When no percentage allocations are set among multiple beneficiaries, the policy defaults to equal sharing. This rule operates independently of whether the policyholder can later modify beneficiaries, though such modifications might influence future distributions.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">When multiple beneficiaries are named without specific allocations, the policy distributes proceeds equally—this applies only if those beneficiaries are alive. Otherwise, the process shifts to the contingency plan outlined in fact_sentence_2, where funds go to the contingent beneficiary.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary disclaims their portion of life insurance proceeds, they are legally considered to have predeceased the insured, meaning their share is excluded from equal division among living beneficiaries.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">When multiple beneficiaries are named without specified shares, the proceeds are split equally; if one such beneficiary cannot be located within 90 days, the company may redirect those funds to the estate instead.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional outcome</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary survives the insured, the insurer considers alternative disbursement paths such as paying the estate. This follows from the rule that unallocated shares among survivors would lead to equal division—but since none survive, the focus shifts to estate payout.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">When multiple beneficiaries are named without defined shares, proceeds are split equally; however, if none survive the insured, the insurer may distribute funds to whomever it deems fairly entitled.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">When multiple beneficiaries are named without specified shares, the proceeds split equally; this triggers payment to the estate, which then satisfies the insurer’s legal responsibility under the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">How the death benefit is divided among beneficiaries relies on the pre-defined amount set forth in the policy specifications, making the distribution rule dependent on the initial determination of payables.</data>
  <data key="d4">informs</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1e3d86f2-cdad-4c30-a947-fc027412cc48" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">receives equal share</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dbe7d497-d868-4da1-95d0-620f81a79fec" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">applies equal share distribution rule</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9cb4ce7f-9577-4146-abdc-61f07ee5f5f7" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">subject of distribution rule</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="d6c09e5a-4a90-493a-be7c-df28237328fe">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="3963c988-4188-4f70-9015-c58e7d5e3661">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="e7bb467c-9044-4a24-a862-0171a1d6246d">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="7637837d-7a12-4d16-b90a-60a7f337a4a1">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="c7a7802c-6a7a-45a1-9e11-4a79eed9a2bd">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">organization</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="edd00972-dcad-4153-981c-bab8488fcbc0">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="adc0a98e-0c4d-4a76-94a5-705d4550adc4">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="7d3fa7ae-4bf9-4618-99e9-eda4a9098ef2">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="636a821f-b1b0-469a-bde3-1ac6371d45de">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">date</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">The ability to change beneficiaries hinges on recognizing their defined role; without defining who the beneficiary is, such modifications would lack context or purpose.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">The right to change a beneficiary applies only if a prior written designation exists. Without such documentation, modifications cannot take place under standard terms.</data>
  <data key="d4">required for</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d3">The policyowner generally retains the right to update beneficiaries unless they are irrevocable. This authority affects how benefit splits are determined, including scenarios where equal division occurs due to unspecified shares.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">The ability to change a beneficiary, as permitted under the policy owner’s control, only becomes active once a formal written request is executed. This ensures procedural clarity in updating beneficiary designations.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">The ability to change a beneficiary at any time while the insured is alive enables updates even after death, so long as the insurer documents the change. This ensures continuity in benefit distribution despite the insured’s passing.</data>
  <data key="d4">extends</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">The capacity to modify beneficiaries ensures that a current, valid primary beneficiary can exist, directly affecting whether the contingent beneficiary becomes relevant upon the insured's death.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 establishes the general rule allowing the policy owner to change beneficiaries unless restricted. Fact_sentence_2 modifies this rule by introducing an exception when an irrevocable beneficiary is named, requiring their consent for any change.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 establishes that beneficiaries can typically be changed unless they are irrevocable. This sets up the condition under which fact_sentence_2 applies—when an irrevocable beneficiary exists, changing or assigning the policy becomes restricted.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">The ability to modify beneficiaries allows the policy owner to ensure a contingent beneficiary remains valid; without such updates, the lack of a surviving beneficiary leads to payout to the estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary is revocable, the policyholder can change them anytime; however, if both the insured and beneficiary die simultaneously, the system assumes the beneficiary died first regardless of intent, ensuring clarity in distribution even without clear evidence of death order.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">The flexibility to modify beneficiaries allows the policy owner to ensure a current beneficiary is named, reducing the likelihood that proceeds default to the estate due to lack of survivors.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary disclaims the proceeds, it effectively removes them from consideration as a recipient, triggering rules about succession based on prior death assumptions. This outcome aligns with the principle stated in fact_sentence_1 regarding flexibility in changing beneficiaries, but applies only when such discretion does not exist due to irrevocability.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">The flexibility to modify beneficiaries allows the policy owner to ensure updated designations; however, if no qualifying beneficiary remains after the insured’s death, the insurer may distribute proceeds based on equity considerations.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">The ability to modify beneficiaries (as stated in fact_sentence_1) sets up the context for scenarios where a named beneficiary might become invalid or unlocatable, leading to the default distribution to the estate outlined in fact_sentence_2.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional outcome</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">If the policy owner changes or removes a beneficiary (unless it's irrevocable), the lack of a valid beneficiary triggers payout procedures to the estate or qualified individuals, leading to the company being discharged from further obligation upon such payment.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditions payment</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">Beneficiary designation affects how the pre-defined death benefit is disbursed; however, the actual amount remains fixed per the Policy Specifications regardless of beneficiary choice.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="3af0e3a5-6ae4-44d2-9986-8f2a02aac638">
  <data key="d3">The rule allowing changes to beneficiaries applies directly to the situation described in fact_sentence_1, meaning Taylor Ellis’s status as beneficiary could be altered unless protected by an irrevocable clause.</data>
  <data key="d4">applies to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="df71832b-c637-4428-9661-6f164f3b5b9f">
  <data key="d3">The general rule allowing changes to beneficiaries applies specifically to Jordan Ellis’s policy, where Taylor Ellis is currently listed as the beneficiary; however, this right does not apply if the beneficiary designation were irrevocable, which would protect her claim regardless of future decisions by the policy owner.</data>
  <data key="d4">applies to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="26fb45c6-1b02-446d-aa67-f95ab9f3bac0">
  <data key="d3">The ability to change beneficiaries unless they are irrevocable relies on applicable state laws; since New York governs this policy, its specific rules on beneficiary modifications determine whether the general principle stated in fact_sentence_2 holds true here.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="49ede6cd-0ba5-4ff3-af79-5ed128947216">
  <data key="d3">While fact_sentence_1 identifies Taylor Ellis as the current beneficiary, fact_sentence_2 clarifies that such designations can be modified unless irrevocable, meaning the initial assignment is subject to future change.</data>
  <data key="d4">informs</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 highlights the policyholder’s power to modify beneficiaries unless they are irrevocable, ensuring that the contingency plan described in fact_sentence_1 remains relevant only if properly set up through current designations.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293" target="9f7028fc-d597-413b-ae31-675f57f9e024">
  <data key="d3">Without the option to name or alter beneficiaries (as stated in fact_sentence_2), the scenario described in fact_sentence_1—where no survivor remains—is more likely to occur due to lack of updated designations.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3963c988-4188-4f70-9015-c58e7d5e3661" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">is subject to change by</data>
</edge>
<edge source="7637837d-7a12-4d16-b90a-60a7f337a4a1" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">can change</data>
</edge>
<edge source="7d3fa7ae-4bf9-4618-99e9-eda4a9098ef2" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">prevents beneficiary change</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0" target="4fc540eb-3bb5-4ff3-8b10-ce4986375a67">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0" target="cdf9e6e6-6ab2-4f3b-b548-619f67f6fbbc">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
