  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="5baeab36-d94a-4eb8-93b9-2d2e9bffc357">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">date</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">Since a beneficiary is defined as the recipient of the death benefit, any modification to this status—even after the insured’s passing—is permissible under policy rules, maintaining administrative continuity.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">Even though the insured is deceased, recording a beneficiary change with the company makes it effective only because such changes require prior written documentation, reinforcing the necessity established in the original designation rule.</data>
  <data key="d4">required by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">Even though the insured has passed away, a beneficiary change remains valid upon official recording by the insurer, confirming that prior authority granted under fact_sentence_1 applies retroactively.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">A beneficiary change is binding from the moment the request is signed, so recording it posthumously does not invalidate it; thus, the change’s validity hinges entirely on prior execution, not the insured’s life status during recordation.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on signing date</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d3">The effectiveness of a beneficiary change after the insured's death depends on whether the beneficiary is revocable; if irrevocable, such changes require prior consent.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d3">Changing a beneficiary remains effective after the insured's death, regardless of timing; however, such changes cannot occur unilaterally if the beneficiary is irrevocable, which introduces a restriction that overrides general procedural validity.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">The ability to change beneficiaries after the insured's death ensures that the contingent beneficiary designation remains relevant and enforceable, enabling proper payout according to the policy terms when no primary beneficiary survives.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">The ability to update beneficiaries after the insured's death ensures that intended distributions remain enforceable; however, if no qualifying beneficiary remains alive, the policy proceeds default to the estate as outlined in the second statement.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">The ability to change a beneficiary posthumously ensures flexibility in administration, but such changes remain subject to legal presumptions about death order, like those outlined here, which prioritize the insurer’s assumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">Changing a beneficiary remains effective after the insured's death, regardless of the time of recording. This rule operates independently of the legal presumption regarding the order of death between the insured and beneficiary.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">The effectiveness of a beneficiary change after the insured's death determines whether proceeds bypass the estate. This ensures that if a new beneficiary is designated before death or during administration, the estate does not receive payment.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">Changing a beneficiary remains valid even after the insured's death, ensuring that designated individuals receive funds regardless of timing. This supports the process leading up to potential estate disbursement if no viable beneficiary is identified within 90 days.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">The ability to update beneficiaries post-mortem ensures administrative control, enabling the insurer to apply equitable principles later when determining rightful recipients among non-surviving parties.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary is changed after the insured’s death but before payout, the company may pay the estate instead, since the original beneficiary is invalid. This triggers the release of the company’s liability upon such payment.</data>
  <data key="d4">implies consequence</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 enables updates to beneficiary designations post-mortem, which directly influences whether the scenario in fact_sentence_1—the absence of a surviving primary beneficiary—is triggered, thereby affecting the allocation path defined in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d" target="9f7028fc-d597-413b-ae31-675f57f9e024">
  <data key="d3">If a new beneficiary is successfully named after the insured’s death (fact_sentence_2), this overrides the condition in fact_sentence_1 requiring a surviving beneficiary, thus altering the path of benefit disbursement.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5394598f-78f2-465e-8e47-2b1d94df4080" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">has beneficiary change applied posthumously</data>
</edge>
<edge source="30e115a9-00b1-4197-a7ee-a64c1cf59b85" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">is designated via change effective after death</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f3de14c3-0bd3-44f4-9203-5e3e53cbb178" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">initiates beneficiary change</data>
</edge>
