  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="05107f99-642d-45b3-be99-9ac9967558c7">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="e553b0d3-5dbb-490a-b4ed-e1c068846dea">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">date</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for written consent when changing an irrevocable beneficiary reinforces that such individuals hold a protected status, consistent with being formally designated recipients of the death benefit as described in the initial statement.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">The rule that an irrevocable beneficiary cannot be changed without consent presupposes that such a designation was formally documented and filed with the insurer, as required by the initial condition.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 imposes a restriction on changing beneficiaries when one is irrevocable, directly limiting the flexibility described in fact_sentence_1. This creates a condition under which the general right to modify beneficiaries does not apply.</data>
  <data key="d4">limits</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">An irrevocable beneficiary status overrides the typical timeline for beneficiary changes, requiring prior consent before any modification—even after a written request is signed.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditions</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">An irrevocable beneficiary status overrides the general rule allowing post-death beneficiary changes, requiring explicit consent before any modification.</data>
  <data key="d4">limits</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 states that changing the beneficiary requires consent if it is irrevocable. This directly informs fact_sentence_2 by establishing that any significant transfer involving the beneficiary—such as assignment—also demands the same consent, reinforcing consistent protection under the irrevocability clause.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">Changing an irrevocable beneficiary requires consent, reinforcing the importance of clear designation; however, if the beneficiary is presumed to have predeceased the insured due to uncertainty in death order, such designations become irrelevant regardless of revocability.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">The restriction on changing an irrevocable beneficiary ensures that even if the policyholder attempts to alter designations, the original beneficiary remains protected. This increases the likelihood that the estate receives proceeds only if no living beneficiary—including an irrevocable one—is present after the insured’s death.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditions for payment</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">The inability to alter an irrevocable beneficiary affects whether a proper beneficiary remains available, potentially leading to delayed identification and triggering estate payment after 90 days.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional outcome</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">The inability to alter an irrevocable beneficiary may result in the death benefit being directed to the estate, triggering the conditions under which the insurer can discharge its obligations through such payments.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">While the death benefit amount is determined by the policy terms, the presence of an irrevocable beneficiary restricts modifications to the payee, ensuring the pre-defined payout goes to the correct party regardless of ownership changes.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b">
  <data key="d3">An irrevocable beneficiary prevents changes to the beneficiary designation, thereby preserving the chain of succession outlined in the provision that directs proceeds to a contingent beneficiary if the primary one doesn’t survive.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="9f7028fc-d597-413b-ae31-675f57f9e024">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a contingent beneficiary clause depends on stable beneficiary designations; however, if those designations cannot be altered due to irrevocable status, it ensures continuity in the chain of eligibility outlined in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="00afa11e-1a32-400d-a440-38b9111578d0">
  <data key="d3">The restriction on changing irrevocable beneficiaries relies on clear contractual terms, which are preserved by the severability principle outlined in fact_sentence_2, ensuring such rules remain effective even if other parts of the policy face legal challenges.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="92a0c3fd-b6dc-4a3b-852d-2dde74d5850f">
  <data key="d3">The restriction on changing an irrevocable beneficiary aligns with the broader principle that policy provisions should remain effective even if some elements fail, reinforcing stability in benefit distribution.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6" target="66d34f7f-f03b-44b9-ae46-16416415bc2a">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for written consent in changing an irrevocable beneficiary aligns with the broader principle that policy modifications—including waivers—must be documented in writing to be valid.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0c20cc25-c05f-46e5-9b76-c25ad8d243c0" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">provides consent for beneficiary change</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a30a90fb-b762-4093-836b-0bd3d164245b" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">requires consent from</data>
</edge>
<edge source="05107f99-642d-45b3-be99-9ac9967558c7" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">requires consensus for beneficiary change</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="e842b725-f7bf-44bc-9ef3-82309d9f3382">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="00cfe007-19e7-4aea-9f2f-08c44a7c60d6">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="99dcdd49-8f0f-4d55-a93c-0cfcc962f174">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="b57e0d69-af5f-44e2-b314-20d14d66005d">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="525113ab-4606-4a0c-b80b-db61fd768dde">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">organization</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="7c457742-2c5c-4294-888e-b21769023803">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="92405263-fbcd-4121-8c0f-066a03677087">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="531a5e86-6d2a-4d43-8d7e-b7de01fcf088">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="26eb2787-e6d9-4976-a920-38a6a3463079">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">date</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">Because a beneficiary is defined as the recipient of the death benefit, naming one as irrevocable imposes binding obligations on the policy owner, requiring their consent before any transfer or change of ownership occurs.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">An irrevocable beneficiary status only applies if the initial designation was properly documented in writing and filed with the insurer; thus, the condition in fact_sentence_2 relies directly on the procedural step outlined in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">When an irrevocable beneficiary is named, the restriction imposed by fact_sentence_2 overrides the general right stated in fact_sentence_1, meaning the policy owner loses unilateral control over beneficiary designation.</data>
  <data key="d4">limits</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for written consent from an irrevocable beneficiary prevents unilateral changes even if a signed request has been submitted, thus restricting the effectiveness of a beneficiary update.</data>
  <data key="d4">restricts</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for written consent from an irrevocable beneficiary limits the ability to modify beneficiaries, meaning that even if a change were attempted posthumously, it would still require prior agreement under irrevocable terms.</data>
  <data key="d4">limits</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 specifies that assigning the policy requires the irrevocable beneficiary’s consent. This supports fact_sentence_1 by extending the requirement of consent beyond mere changes to beneficiaries, showing that broader transfers are equally restricted to preserve the beneficiary’s status.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">An irrevocable beneficiary cannot be removed without consent, ensuring stability in designation; however, if the beneficiary is presumed to have died before the insured due to simultaneous deaths, the designated beneficiary would not receive proceeds regardless of irrevocability.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for consent from an irrevocable beneficiary limits changes to the policy, but regardless of such constraints, if no beneficiary survives the insured, the proceeds still default to the estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for consent when altering a policy with an irrevocable beneficiary ensures that such changes cannot occur unilaterally. This rule supports the integrity of beneficiary designations, including those whose disclaimers trigger legal presumptions about death timing.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for irrevocable beneficiary consent limits changes to the policy, potentially leading to situations where no living beneficiary receives proceeds, thus triggering payout to the estate. This scenario aligns with the conditions under which insurers may pay the estate, as noted in fact_sentence_2.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional outcome</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b">
  <data key="d3">An irrevocable beneficiary cannot be removed without consent, affecting whether proceeds flow to them or default to contingents/estate. This impacts the applicability of the contingency provision described in fact_sentence_1 by preserving certain beneficiary rights even if the original designation might otherwise lapse.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="92a0c3fd-b6dc-4a3b-852d-2dde74d5850f">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for irrevocable beneficiary consent in assigning a policy is preserved and upheld by the general principle of contract durability outlined in the second fact.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="66d34f7f-f03b-44b9-ae46-16416415bc2a">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for written consent in assigning a policy due to an irrevocable beneficiary complements the broader rule requiring written waivers for any policy changes, reinforcing consistent formalities across policy modifications.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df" target="a2f514f9-483a-46e4-bd60-a528a1b42c8b">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for written consent from an irrevocable beneficiary aligns with broader contractual formality standards emphasized in the second statement, reinforcing that changes to policy status require explicit authorization.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="00cfe007-19e7-4aea-9f2f-08c44a7c60d6" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">provides consent for assignment</data>
</edge>
<edge source="b57e0d69-af5f-44e2-b314-20d14d66005d" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">requires consent from</data>
</edge>
<edge source="531a5e86-6d2a-4d43-8d7e-b7de01fcf088" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">requires consent for policy assignment</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="4bcdc3cd-dcb7-45df-bdd6-373e80d4b40e">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="0bc4ca10-c6d0-4f07-81bb-0e8f70db69f8">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="5b02c028-cca1-475d-98c6-5ceaec92ccb8">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="c5831b50-88cc-4d8e-9fd5-d156a2783d27">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">person</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="e3476275-8b7a-4dd7-bafb-468a1b37bd2f">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">organization</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="350db99e-8ab0-4a67-af52-2860aa96e421">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="27957a26-1dbb-4500-b0b2-78e69f18332e">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="2d4b5733-382f-44d1-bae1-d16099528ed3">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">concept</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="3c646a59-5078-4772-93c2-321639797a26">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">date</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">If the primary beneficiary predeceases the insured, the contingency plan outlined in sentence two depends entirely on the existence and validity of such a beneficiary, making it dependent on the initial designation described in sentence one.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">The payment to a contingent beneficiary hinges on having a formally established primary beneficiary through a written filing with the company.</data>
  <data key="d4">required for</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d3">If no primary beneficiary survives the insured, the payment flows to the contingent beneficiary; however, if that fails, the rule from fact_sentence_1 would apply only if multiple beneficiaries were originally named and survived—but since none did, it becomes irrelevant.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">If no primary beneficiary survives, the contingency plan activates—requiring prior designation of a contingent beneficiary—which depends on the initial choice and potential changes allowed by the policy owner.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">When the primary beneficiary predeceases the insured, payment flows to the contingent beneficiary—a scenario that underscores why timely beneficiary changes (as noted in fact_sentence_1) are essential to ensure intended recipients receive funds.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
