<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">If it is legally presumed that the beneficiary predeceased the insured due to simultaneous death, this assumption relies on the prior understanding that beneficiaries are individuals designated to receive the death benefit upon the insured’s passing.</data>
  <data key="d4">assumed predecessor</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">If the insurer assumes the beneficiary predeceased the insured due to simultaneous deaths, this implies the original written designation was necessary for that legal framework to apply.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">The assumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured applies only when the beneficiary is revocable—if it were irrevocable, changes would require consent, making such assumptions less relevant due to stricter control over designation.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">When it is unclear whether the insured or beneficiary died first, the company assumes the beneficiary died earlier, which reinforces why a beneficiary change recorded after the insured’s death still holds effect only if consistent with this legal framework.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">The legal assumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured justifies why proceeds would pass to the contingent beneficiary instead of being voided due to lack of survivorship.</data>
  <data key="d4">preempts</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">The legal presumption that the beneficiary died before the insured justifies directing proceeds to the estate when no viable beneficiary remains, aligning with the rule that estates receive payouts if no qualifying beneficiary survives.</data>
  <data key="d4">justifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">If the insured and beneficiary die at the same time, the insurer assumes the beneficiary died first—unless state law specifies differently.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 1 states the company assumes the beneficiary predeceased the insured in case of simultaneous or uncertain death. Fact sentence 2 reinforces this by stating the beneficiary is legally presumed to have predeceased the insured under similar conditions.</data>
  <data key="d4">equivalent to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">1.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">When the insured and beneficiary die simultaneously, the company presumes the beneficiary predeceased the insured, allowing the proceeds to bypass the beneficiary entirely and proceed to the estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">If the insured and beneficiary die at the same time, the insurer assumes the beneficiary died first. This assumption carries over even if the beneficiary later disclaims the benefit, treating them as though they had already predeceased the insured.</data>
  <data key="d4">extends predecessor</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">When the beneficiary is legally presumed to have died before the insured due to simultaneous deaths, the company has discretion to distribute proceeds to anyone considered equitably entitled, ensuring payment even without a valid beneficiary.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">If it is legally presumed that the beneficiary predeceased the insured due to simultaneous deaths, then the insurer may pay the death benefit to the estate or an equitable claimant, thereby fulfilling its obligations.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">When the insured and beneficiary die simultaneously, the company assumes the beneficiary predeceased the insured, triggering alternative payout pathways. If no beneficiary is found within 90 days after the insured’s death, the proceeds may be paid to the estate, reflecting a procedural continuation from the initial presumption.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f">
  <data key="d3">When the beneficiary is legally presumed to have died before the insured due to simultaneous deaths, the distribution path shifts away from traditional beneficiaries toward equitable recipients. This opens the possibility for individuals who paid for the insured’s final care or funeral to receive benefits.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">When the beneficiary is legally presumed to have predeceased the insured due to uncertain timing of deaths, the death benefit still applies per the Policy Specifications; thus, the stated amount remains binding regardless of beneficiary status.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="3af0e3a5-6ae4-44d2-9986-8f2a02aac638">
  <data key="d3">The legal assumption that the beneficiary predeceases the insured only matters because Taylor Ellis is designated as the beneficiary, making this provision essential for determining payout outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d4">informs</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="df71832b-c637-4428-9661-6f164f3b5b9f">
  <data key="d3">The legal presumption that the beneficiary predeceases the insured clarifies the outcome of the beneficiary relationship established in fact_sentence_1, ensuring proper handling of the policy proceeds even if Taylor and Jordan die simultaneously.</data>
  <data key="d4">informs</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="26fb45c6-1b02-446d-aa67-f95ab9f3bac0">
  <data key="d3">The provision on simultaneous death relies on New York State law—established in fact_sentence_1—to determine the default assumption about the order of death, making the governing law essential for applying this rule.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="49ede6cd-0ba5-4ff3-af79-5ed128947216">
  <data key="d3">The legal presumption in fact_sentence_2 depends directly on identifying the beneficiary, as stated in fact_sentence_1. It determines whether the death benefit flows to the estate or another party if the beneficiary’s survival status is unproven.</data>
  <data key="d4">informs</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 introduces a legal presumption regarding survivorship in ambiguous death scenarios, which clarifies when the condition in fact_sentence_1—that no designated beneficiary survives—is triggered, thereby enabling smooth execution of the payout sequence.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="9f7028fc-d597-413b-ae31-675f57f9e024">
  <data key="d3">The rule that a beneficiary is presumed to have predeceased the insured in case of simultaneous death ensures that the condition in fact_sentence_1—that no surviving beneficiary exists—is more likely to be met, thus activating alternative payout mechanisms.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91" target="92a0c3fd-b6dc-4a3b-852d-2dde74d5850f">
  <data key="d3">If the beneficiary is presumed to have predeceased the insured due to simultaneous deaths, the policy’s distribution mechanism remains intact only if other provisions—including those governing legal resilience—are still effective.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
