<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">If the beneficiary is presumed to have died before the insured, they do not qualify to receive the death benefit, making the initial definition of the beneficiary irrelevant in such scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">If the beneficiary is presumed to have predeceased the insured due to simultaneous death, the need for a formally documented beneficiary designation becomes critical to avoid distribution to the estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">Legal assumptions about the order of death—such as assuming the beneficiary died first—can nullify the intended effects of a recently submitted beneficiary change.</data>
  <data key="d4">overrides</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">The legal presumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured influences how claims are settled, especially when determining eligibility for benefit disbursement. This presumption does not override an already-recorded beneficiary change, assuming it was properly executed before death.</data>
  <data key="d4">applies to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d3">Even if a beneficiary is irrevocable, the legal presumption that they predeceased the insured overrides the original intent, potentially nullifying the need for consent in altering distributions based on death sequence.</data>
  <data key="d4">preempts</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d3">When it is legally presumed that the beneficiary predeceased the insured, even an irrevocable beneficiary loses entitlement, meaning the requirement for written consent becomes irrelevant because the beneficiary is deemed ineligible under the presumption.</data>
  <data key="d4">supersedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">The legal principle that a beneficiary is presumed to have died before the insured enables the transfer of proceeds to the contingent beneficiary when no living primary beneficiary exists.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">The legal presumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured justifies directing proceeds to the estate when no qualifying beneficiary remains after the initial payout chain fails.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditions</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">State laws can override the standard assumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured in cases of simultaneous death.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">The legal presumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured creates the condition under which the insurer pays proceeds to the estate instead of an individual beneficiary.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
