<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary disclaims the life insurance proceeds, they are legally considered to have died before the insured, consistent with the general presumption established in the first sentence.</data>
  <data key="d4">applies to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 sets forth the default legal presumption that beneficiaries predecease the insured, except where state law differs. This principle directly informs the application described in fact_sentence_2, where uncertainty about death order triggers the same presumption.</data>
  <data key="d4">applies to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">If the beneficiary is presumed to have predeceased the insured under state law, and no other eligible beneficiary is identified within 90 days, the life insurance proceeds may be paid to the insured’s estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">When the beneficiary is legally presumed to have died before the insured due to simultaneous death or uncertainty, the absence of a valid beneficiary triggers alternate payment procedures. This creates the condition under which the insurer may distribute proceeds to individuals it deems equitably entitled.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f">
  <data key="d3">If it cannot be determined whether the beneficiary outlived the insured, the law presumes the beneficiary died first. This opens the door for equitable claims by others, including those who paid for the insured’s final medical care or funeral.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">If the beneficiary is legally presumed to have died before the insured due to simultaneous death or uncertainty, the life insurance proceeds are directed to the estate. This triggers the company’s ability to pay the estate or an equitable recipient, thereby discharging its liability.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">Whether the beneficiary is deemed to have predeceased the insured impacts the allocation of the death benefit, whose exact amount was already established in the policy specifications.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b">
  <data key="d3">The legal presumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured justifies the application of the contingent beneficiary provision outlined in fact_sentence_1, ensuring orderly payout when primary beneficiaries cannot claim.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="9f7028fc-d597-413b-ae31-675f57f9e024">
  <data key="d3">The legal presumption that a beneficiary predeceased the insured determines whether proceeds flow to contingent beneficiaries or the estate, aligning with the condition described in fact_sentence_1 regarding non-survival.</data>
  <data key="d4">informs</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="92a0c3fd-b6dc-4a3b-852d-2dde74d5850f">
  <data key="d3">The presumption about the beneficiary predeceasing the insured functions within the broader framework of policy enforceability described in the second statement, ensuring such rules persist even if parts of the contract face challenges.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33" target="00afa11e-1a32-400d-a440-38b9111578d0">
  <data key="d3">The presumption about the beneficiary predeceasing the insured relies on legal frameworks that could potentially be challenged or modified; this clause ensures such challenges do not invalidate the entire policy framework governing benefit distribution.</data>
  <data key="d4">severability support</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
