<edge source="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">When the order of death between the insured and beneficiary is unclear, fact_sentence_2 invokes the presumption established in fact_sentence_1—that the beneficiary is deemed to have died before the insured—unless state law specifies otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d4">derives from</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">When the order of death is uncertain, the beneficiary is presumed to have died before the insured, removing them from eligibility. As a result, the insurer may distribute the proceeds to the insured’s estate, fulfilling the payout obligation.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">When the order of death is uncertain, the beneficiary is presumed to have died before the insured. This presumption holds even if the beneficiary later disclaims the proceeds, treating them as though they had predeceased the insured.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">When the beneficiary is presumed to have predeceased the insured due to uncertainty in death order, the death benefit becomes payable to the estate or an equitable claimant, thereby fulfilling the insurer’s duty.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">When the order of death is uncertain, the beneficiary is presumed to have died before the insured, eliminating them as a valid recipient. As a result, the insurer gains authority to award proceeds to anyone considered equitably entitled, such as those who paid for final expenses.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5" target="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f">
  <data key="d3">When the order of death is uncertain, the beneficiary is presumed to have died first, triggering alternative payout paths. Under such conditions, persons who paid for the insured’s final care or funeral may be considered equitably entitled to receive benefits.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">When the order of death between the insured and beneficiary is ambiguous, the beneficiary is presumed to have died first, clearing the way for estate distribution. This aligns with the scenario in fact_sentence_2, where failure to identify a living beneficiary leads directly to estate payout.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">The rule on beneficiary precedence depends on knowing the death benefit amount to determine whether distributions occur at all, linking procedural assumptions to financial obligations outlined in the policy specs.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
