<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary disclaims the proceeds, they are deemed to have died before the insured, meaning they cannot inherit; thus, the original definition of a beneficiary becomes irrelevant in such cases due to the legal consequence of disclaimer.</data>
  <data key="d4">predecedes → invalidates claim</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">For a beneficiary to disclaim proceeds and trigger the legal assumption of predecease, they must first have been formally named via a written submission to the insurer.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditions on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d3">The rule that proceeds are split equally among unnamed percentage beneficiaries depends on active beneficiaries; thus, a disclaiming beneficiary ceases to qualify for such an equal share due to being deemed to have predeceased the insured.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">The ability to change beneficiaries (as per fact_sentence_1) underscores why disclaimers matter—they allow a beneficiary to opt out without being bound by the original designation, thereby enabling the transfer of benefits according to contingency plans outlined in the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary disclaims proceeds, they are deemed to have predeceased the insured, affecting distribution pathways. This outcome aligns with the principle that only designated beneficiaries receive funds, reinforcing why irrevocable beneficiaries require explicit consent before any change.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary disclaims proceeds, they are deemed to have predeceased the insured, meaning they do not qualify as a surviving beneficiary, so the proceeds proceed to the contingent beneficiary as per standard protocol.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary disclaims the proceeds, they are treated as though they died before the insured, meaning they cannot receive the death benefit. As a result, the funds would follow the next tier in the distribution hierarchy, potentially leading to payment to the policy owner’s estate, as specified in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary disclaims the proceeds, it triggers the same legal treatment as if they had predeceased the insured, aligning with the initial presumption established when death order is ambiguous.</data>
  <data key="d4">applies rule</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">The legal presumption that a beneficiary predeceased the insured—unless overridden by state law—is applied specifically when a beneficiary disclaims the proceeds, making such a disclaimer equivalent to prior death.</data>
  <data key="d4">extends</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5">
  <data key="d3">The legal treatment of a disclaimer implies that the beneficiary is considered to have predeceased the insured, reinforcing the general rule applied when death order is ambiguous.</data>
  <data key="d4">applies rule</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary disclaims the proceeds, they are deemed to have predeceased the insured, eliminating them as an eligible recipient. As a result, if no other qualified beneficiaries exist, the insurer may distribute the proceeds to the insured’s estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">predecedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary disclaims proceeds, they are deemed to have predeceased the insured, potentially triggering estate distribution. This aligns with scenario 2, where lack of identifiable beneficiaries leads to estate payout after 90 days.</data>
  <data key="d4">similar outcome</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary disclaims proceeds, it triggers the transfer of funds to the estate, leading to payment being made to the estate or an equitable claimant, thereby fulfilling the insurer’s duty.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary disclaims the proceeds, they are legally considered to have predeceased the insured, potentially opening up eligibility for alternate claimants like those who paid for final care or burial.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary disclaims proceeds, the total amount payable under the policy remains unchanged; however, its allocation shifts based on successor claims, consistent with the policy-specified death benefit framework.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3123cde6-17c7-4ead-8bee-ebb4ae04ea47" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">has beneficiary predeceased treatment</data>
</edge>
