</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">When the insurer pays proceeds to the estate due to an unlocatable beneficiary, it reflects that the original beneficiary designation specified in the first sentence was not fulfilled, necessitating alternative distribution.</data>
  <data key="d4">failure of designation</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">When beneficiaries fail to be identified or contacted within 90 days after the insured’s death, the need arises for formal documentation of beneficiary status, underscoring why such designations must be documented in writing.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary cannot be located within 90 days, the insurer may pay the proceeds to the estate, especially relevant when equal division applies due to unspecified share allocations among multiple beneficiaries.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary cannot be located within 90 days (per fact_sentence_2), it implies that prior arrangements like revocable beneficiary designations could have been altered or were ineffective, reinforcing the importance of having clear designation protocols discussed in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary is found within 90 days, proceeds are paid to the estate—a fallback mechanism triggered only if prior steps like beneficiary updates fail or aren’t applicable.</data>
  <data key="d4">sequential precondition</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d3">When a beneficiary isn't found within 90 days, the estate receives proceeds—a scenario that could arise if attempts to contact or update an irrevocable beneficiary fail due to lack of consent.</data>
  <data key="d4">prevents alteration</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">Proceeds may be paid to the estate if a beneficiary isn't located within 90 days. This only occurs after exhausting the initial step of paying the contingent beneficiary, should one exist.</data>
  <data key="d4">temporal precedence</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 states that if a beneficiary isn't found within 90 days, proceeds may go to the estate. This complements fact_sentence_1 by offering an alternative path to estate disbursement rooted in procedural delays rather than death sequencing.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">The inability to locate a beneficiary within 90 days leads to payment to the estate, but only after applying the legal presumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured in cases of simultaneous deaths. Thus, the outcome depends on prior assumptions about death order.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary can be located within 90 days after the insured’s death, it triggers the application of the legal presumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured, leading to estate disbursement.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary remains unlocated within 90 days post-insured’s death, proceeds default to the estate. This outcome mirrors the consequence of the legal presumption in fact_sentence_1, where the beneficiary is treated as having predeceased the insured, resulting in similar estate transfer.</data>
  <data key="d4">result of</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">If a beneficiary isn’t found within 90 days, proceeds go to the estate—a result similar to that caused by disclaimer, where the beneficiary is effectively removed from consideration.</data>
  <data key="d4">similar outcome</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary is found within 90 days, proceeds go to the estate; this serves as one pathway leading into the broader principle that unclaimed benefits may instead be awarded to those considered equitably entitled if no beneficiary survived.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 describes a delay-based trigger—payment to the estate occurs if a beneficiary remains unfound after 90 days. This complements fact_sentence_1, which applies when no beneficiary survives at all. While fact_sentence_1 covers immediate absence, fact_sentence_2 introduces a procedural timeline, suggesting a potential progression from temporary non-location to permanent eligibility failure.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary is found within 90 days, proceeds may go to the estate; however, those who incurred qualifying expenses related to the insured’s final illness or burial may also be deemed equitably entitled to receive funds instead.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">When beneficiaries are not located within 90 days, the insurer pays the proceeds to the estate, triggering the release of the company’s liability.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">When proceeds are paid to the estate due to inability to locate a beneficiary within 90 days, the amount disbursed corresponds to the death benefit already established in the Policy Specifications.</data>
  <data key="d4">complies with</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="3af0e3a5-6ae4-44d2-9986-8f2a02aac638">
  <data key="d3">The rule about paying proceeds to the estate if the beneficiary isn’t found within 90 days provides a fallback mechanism consistent with standard insurance practice. Given that Taylor Ellis is the named beneficiary in the original policy, this clause ensures continuity of payout even if contact with the beneficiary fails.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="df71832b-c637-4428-9661-6f164f3b5b9f">
  <data key="d3">The rule that proceeds may go to the estate if a beneficiary isn’t found applies specifically to situations where the named beneficiary (Taylor Ellis) cannot be contacted or verified within 90 days after Jordan Ellis’s death, creating a conditional override to the standard beneficiary designation.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="26fb45c6-1b02-446d-aa67-f95ab9f3bac0">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 outlines a condition—payment to the estate after 90 days—if a beneficiary isn't found, which relies on the governing law stated in fact_sentence_1 to define the validity and procedure of such action.</data>
  <data key="d4">informed by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="49ede6cd-0ba5-4ff3-af79-5ed128947216">
  <data key="d3">If the named beneficiary (Taylor Ellis) cannot be located after the insured’s death, the policy’s original definition of the beneficiary becomes insufficient, triggering alternate payout rules governed by the company’s protocol.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 provides an exception pathway to the estate when beneficiaries cannot be identified, reinforcing the contingency plan established in fact_sentence_1 regarding non-surviving beneficiaries.</data>
  <data key="d4">extends</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="9f7028fc-d597-413b-ae31-675f57f9e024">
  <data key="d3">The condition in fact_sentence_2—that proceeds may go to the estate if a beneficiary isn’t found within 90 days—is consistent with the broader principle in fact_sentence_1 that proceeds flow to alternative recipients like the estate when no living beneficiary exists.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="00afa11e-1a32-400d-a440-38b9111578d0">
  <data key="d3">The rule governing estate disbursement due to missing beneficiaries relies on the overall legal framework of the policy, which includes clauses like severability to maintain functionality despite potential challenges.</data>
  <data key="d4">severability support</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="92a0c3fd-b6dc-4a3b-852d-2dde74d5850f">
  <data key="d3">The rule allowing payment to the estate due to inability to locate a beneficiary relies on the overall legal enforceability of the policy terms, which is upheld under the principle stated in fact_sentence_2.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b" target="a2f514f9-483a-46e4-bd60-a528a1b42c8b">
  <data key="d3">The rule about paying proceeds to the estate after 90 days depends on proper administrative handling, which aligns with the broader principle that policy amendments require official approval, reinforcing institutional control over exceptions.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
