<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a formally designated beneficiary determines whether the insurer must follow that designation or instead exercise judgment in awarding proceeds under default conditions.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">When beneficiaries fail to meet eligibility due to lack of proper written designation, the company exercises discretionary power to allocate funds to those considered fairly entitled, reflecting the necessity of formal beneficiary documentation.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d3">The insurer has authority to assign proceeds to an equitable recipient if no beneficiary survives, even though equal sharing among multiple beneficiaries would apply only when survivors exist without designated proportions.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary survives the insured, the absence of a valid recipient triggers provisions allowing payment to those equitably entitled, which depends on prior designation choices—including whether changes were permitted under revocable terms.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary survives, the insurer uses its discretion based on equity; however, prior beneficiary changes remain binding only if executed before death, reinforcing the importance of timely updates.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">The insurer’s authority to designate equitable recipients follows logically from the failure of both primary and contingent beneficiaries, serving as a fallback mechanism when formal designations do not apply.</data>
  <data key="d4">extends</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">The insurer retains authority to assign proceeds to equitable recipients when no beneficiary survives, even though the estate typically inherits under contingency plans outlined in earlier clauses.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">The company’s authority to pay proceeds to an equitably entitled individual arises precisely in situations—such as simultaneous death—where the beneficiary is presumed not to survive the insured.</data>
  <data key="d4">follows from</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">The ability of the insurance company to pay proceeds to persons considered equitably entitled depends on the legal assumption that the beneficiary did not survive the insured. Without this presumption, such discretionary payouts would lack basis.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5">
  <data key="d3">The legal presumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured creates a scenario where no eligible beneficiary remains. Consequently, the insurer may exercise discretion to pay proceeds to individuals deemed equitably entitled, reflecting the practical outcome of the initial presumption.</data>
  <data key="d4">result of</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">The company has discretionary authority to designate an equitable recipient if no beneficiary survives, which includes the possibility of directing funds to the estate as one such option.</data>
  <data key="d4">includes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">The rule permitting payment to anyone deemed equitably entitled applies when no beneficiary survives the insured, providing a fallback mechanism that overlaps conceptually with the 90-day estate payout clause, though it operates on different criteria.</data>
  <data key="d4">extends</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">When proceeds are paid to a person deemed equitably entitled due to lack of surviving beneficiaries, the insurer is released from further obligation up to the amount paid.</data>
  <data key="d4">discharges</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f">
  <data key="d3">When no beneficiary survives, the insurer may pay proceeds to an equitable recipient; one example includes someone who paid for the insured’s medical care or funeral costs.</data>
  <data key="d4">exemplifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">When there is no surviving beneficiary, the payment process relies on the pre-defined death benefit amount outlined in the policy specifications to determine the funds available for transfer.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="1fa01fa6-dfb3-4343-85a2-88951da5f49b">
  <data key="d3">While fact_sentence_2 allows discretionary payout to persons deemed equitably entitled, it aligns with fact_sentence_1’s intent by ensuring proceeds are distributed appropriately when no named beneficiary survives, reinforcing the principle of orderly disbursement.</data>
  <data key="d4">extends</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476" target="9f7028fc-d597-413b-ae31-675f57f9e024">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 introduces a discretionary mechanism allowing the insurer to distribute proceeds to persons it deems equitable recipients, which complements fact_sentence_1’s framework by providing a flexible alternative when traditional beneficiaries fail to survive.</data>
  <data key="d4">extends</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
