<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="63bb501a-4ca1-4c96-97f8-47c7fd6666d6">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">date</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">While fact 2 describes individuals who may be equitably entitled despite lacking formal designation, it relies on the foundational idea from fact 1—that only certain parties (like beneficiaries) are initially recognized to receive benefits.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">Properly documenting a beneficiary ensures clarity in distribution; without it, equity-based claims become relevant, especially for those who bore financial burdens during the insured’s final period.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">Someone who paid for the insured’s final illness or burial may be considered equitably entitled to benefits when no formal beneficiary is available. Such individuals could only receive funds if prior beneficiary designations were invalid or unclaimed—making the timing of any beneficiary update critical.</data>
  <data key="d4">contextual dependency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">Individuals who incurred expenses for the insured’s final care or burial may qualify for payment only after traditional beneficiary pathways, like the contingent beneficiary, have been exhausted or determined invalid.</data>
  <data key="d4">exception to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">Individuals who incurred expenses for the insured’s final care or burial may be granted access to proceeds due to equitable entitlement, especially when no proper beneficiary exists—including when the estate would normally inherit but exceptions allow direct payment.</data>
  <data key="d4">applies to estate</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">The provision allowing payment to persons who incurred expenses during the insured’s final illness or burial relies on the prior assumption that the named beneficiary did not survive the insured. Without this presumption, such claims would lack basis in the distribution hierarchy.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">Individuals who incurred legitimate expenses for the insured’s final illness or burial may qualify for benefits if no proper beneficiary exists. This possibility arises precisely because the beneficiary is presumed to have predeceased the insured under default rules.</data>
  <data key="d4">follows from</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5">
  <data key="d3">The rule that a beneficiary is presumed to have predeceased the insured in ambiguous death scenarios creates a pathway for non-beneficiaries to claim benefits based on equity, including those who covered medical or funeral costs.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">Individuals who incurred expenses for the insured’s final illness or burial may qualify as equitable recipients, especially when other beneficiaries—including those who disclaimed—are not available due to legal presumptions.</data>
  <data key="d4">allows for</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">The possibility of paying someone who incurred expenses from the insured’s final illness or burial arises only after standard beneficiary channels fail—including when proceeds would otherwise go to the estate due to lack of survivors.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">Individuals who incurred expenses for the insured’s final illness or burial may qualify as equitable beneficiaries, potentially receiving proceeds even if no formal beneficiary is identified within 90 days, thus influencing the distribution path from the estate.</data>
  <data key="d4">exemplifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">The inclusion of expense-incurred persons as equitable recipients expands the scope of eligibility under the broader rule allowing payment to those deemed equitably entitled.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
  <data key="d3">If someone has incurred legitimate expenses related to the insured’s final illness or burial, they may qualify as "equitably entitled." Payment to such a person satisfies the company’s duty under the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditions discharge</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">Individuals receiving payment based on expenses from the insured’s final illness or burial do so only up to the amount stipulated in the policy’s death benefit specification, ensuring alignment with contractual obligations.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="00afa11e-1a32-400d-a440-38b9111578d0">
  <data key="d3">The rule allowing payment to individuals who bore end-of-life expenses relies on the broader contractual framework being upheld; if parts of the policy were voided, it could undermine this specific provision.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f" target="92a0c3fd-b6dc-4a3b-852d-2dde74d5850f">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 establishes a specific exception for distributing benefits to persons who paid for end-of-life expenses, while fact_sentence_2 ensures that such exceptions remain enforceable if challenged, preserving the clause’s validity under severability.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
