<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="d680623c-b8e6-4216-87fc-f28fbd801474">
  <data key="d3">When the insurer pays the estate or an individual deemed equitably entitled instead of the primary beneficiary, it confirms that the original designation of the beneficiary has been bypassed, validating the initial definition of the beneficiary’s intended role.</data>
  <data key="d4">replaces</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="75149b06-31da-4902-818b-05639e24c747">
  <data key="d3">When the company pays the estate or an equitable recipient, it does so because no validly designated beneficiary was identified through proper written filing, fulfilling the condition set by the initial rule.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="5c01ae55-a8cf-4383-9489-8098cb74d07d">
  <data key="d3">The insurer’s liability ends upon payment to the estate or an equitable claimant, which becomes relevant only after unequal distributions fail due to unspecified shares, leading to equal division among beneficiaries.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="a2150cee-d022-441d-940f-3a1ca38fb293">
  <data key="d3">The ability to change beneficiaries (except irrevocable ones) directly influences whether funds proceed to the estate; thus, the outcome described in sentence 2 depends on the flexibility allowed in modifying beneficiaries under sentence 1.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables discharge</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="f4dfbce0-ca9c-4a02-8aa7-27e266777ed0">
  <data key="d3">If the insurer pays the estate or an equitable recipient, this action confirms that any prior beneficiary changes were either ineffective or not properly processed; such payment assumes the change was already initiated per proper procedure.</data>
  <data key="d4">justifies action</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="3f213a1e-e278-42fb-8c8b-f3adf267911d">
  <data key="d3">When the company pays the estate or an equitable recipient, it confirms that no valid beneficiary exists, often because a change was made posthumously or no beneficiary survived, thereby validating the effect of late beneficiary updates.</data>
  <data key="d4">justifies change</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="0d942949-f688-4e68-85e8-07b70d1319c6">
  <data key="d3">When the insurer pays the estate or an equitable claimant, it reflects a scenario potentially caused by the existence of an irrevocable beneficiary whose consent prevents modification, leading to default estate payout.</data>
  <data key="d4">result of</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="bc01826e-0790-4744-905a-5c0919e140df">
  <data key="d3">When payment is made to the estate due to unavailability of a beneficiary—possibly because of restrictions like those imposed by an irrevocable beneficiary—the insurer is discharged from further obligations, reinforcing why such designations require formal consent.</data>
  <data key="d4">preceded by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="bca821e4-dd36-4856-bd49-b34de7e5162f">
  <data key="d3">The insurer’s liability ends upon payment to the estate or an equitable recipient, which only occurs after the contingent beneficiary pathway has been exhausted due to lack of eligibility.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="38974002-cdef-417b-abe4-05fe910d1ed4">
  <data key="d3">The insurer’s liability is discharged upon payment to the estate, which only occurs if no contingent beneficiary survives the insured, making the condition precedent dependent on the prior clause.</data>
  <data key="d4">precedes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="ac1a6b74-dc59-4a4f-a24b-16a083744b91">
  <data key="d3">The assumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured allows the insurer to transfer funds to the estate or an equitable party, ensuring the company’s liability is discharged after such payment.</data>
  <data key="d4">discharges</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="57f5cb31-60fc-403b-8faf-61bb58c44d33">
  <data key="d3">When the insurer pays the estate or an equitable claimant, it does so under the assumption that the beneficiary predeceased the insured—a condition established by the legal presumption outlined in the prior statement.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="a1058e7f-8f18-49f2-bc24-6b94e7ac24c5">
  <data key="d3">The insurer’s liability is discharged upon payment to the estate or an equitable recipient only if the beneficiary is legally presumed to have died before the insured, which occurs under ambiguous death scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="927cb107-8365-4668-bb63-e17e86722615">
  <data key="d3">The insurer’s liability ends upon payment to the estate or an equitable recipient, which only occurs after a beneficiary has disclaimed the proceeds, making such disclaimers a prerequisite for this outcome.</data>
  <data key="d4">preceded by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="a768f1b0-5081-44f9-8ca1-4d423f35bb90">
  <data key="d3">The company’s liability is discharged only after proceeds are paid to the estate, which occurs specifically when no living beneficiary is available.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="23a68193-61ae-40df-becb-863cc2fa667b">
  <data key="d3">The company’s liability is discharged upon paying proceeds to the estate, which occurs only after a failed attempt to locate a living beneficiary within 90 days.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="3e91835e-9ad1-4260-b452-07cf78be6476">
  <data key="d3">The insurer’s liability is discharged upon paying proceeds to an equitable recipient, which only occurs when no valid beneficiary remains after the insured’s death.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditions</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="f075e888-0cb1-43c8-8ebf-84c98e1c462f">
  <data key="d3">The insurer’s liability ends upon payment to the estate or an individual deemed equitably entitled, meaning such recipients must meet specific eligibility conditions, including having covered qualifying end-of-life expenses.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables discharge</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="c9a8b525-e466-428a-9f1b-a7e27067ae66">
  <data key="d3">Knowing that payment to the estate or an equitable party fully discharges the insurer’s duty clarifies how the fixed death benefit from the policy is ultimately fulfilled and settled.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="6364d71d-2969-4592-8deb-8bc2dc62cbbd">
  <data key="d3">The rule governing payment to the estate (fact_sentence_1) applies only once the death benefit has been triggered; fact_sentence_2 ensures that after two years from reinstatement, such benefits become fully payable without restriction, enabling proper execution of the distribution clause.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="00afa11e-1a32-400d-a440-38b9111578d0">
  <data key="d3">The rule on estate payments complements the severability clause by ensuring that even if certain beneficiary designations are invalidated, the overall policy framework—including payout mechanisms—remains functional.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="66d34f7f-f03b-44b9-ae46-16416415bc2a">
  <data key="d3">The rule about discharging liability upon payment to the estate relies on clear contractual procedures, which are upheld by requiring written waivers for any changes to policy terms.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="92a0c3fd-b6dc-4a3b-852d-2dde74d5850f">
  <data key="d3">The rule governing discharge of liability upon payment to the estate relies on the overall contract integrity ensured by the severability provision, meaning its enforceability hinges on the remainder of the policy being upheld.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf" target="a2f514f9-483a-46e4-bd60-a528a1b42c8b">
  <data key="d3">The rule on discharging liability via payment to the estate relies on formal procedures, which are reinforced by the requirement that any waiver of policy terms must be formally documented and approved—an implicit safeguard against unauthorized alterations to payout mechanisms.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2efbac7b-4ba3-4c19-87b4-1897c3dda106" target="91d5242d-2ab5-43dd-a6c9-1df492a48caf">
