</edge>
<edge source="6c958eff-bcba-442c-aeee-865d50cfdb4e" target="c5189b85-3b6b-486e-aed3-92e4fdeec68a">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 specifies New York courts as the default venue for resolving disputes, but fact_sentence_2 qualifies this by permitting alternative venues when required by applicable law, thereby limiting the enforceability of the initial jurisdictional rule.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="6c958eff-bcba-442c-aeee-865d50cfdb4e" target="3063337b-6bf5-4d8f-be93-1c56bd525563">
  <data key="d3">The clause specifying New York courts for dispute resolution relies on the broader principle that remaining provisions remain effective, ensuring its enforceability within the policy framework.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="6c958eff-bcba-442c-aeee-865d50cfdb4e" target="80c83885-daf8-494c-ba9e-82a540a78e41">
  <data key="d3">The specified venue for dispute resolution in New York provides context for interpreting the enforceability clause, ensuring consistency with state law when assessing invalid provisions.</data>
  <data key="d4">supported by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ff7323a2-3f4c-4fcc-a5ee-273dde08ec2f" target="6c958eff-bcba-442c-aeee-865d50cfdb4e">
